diff --git a/.grove/53-impl--linux-download-consistency-k14.md b/.grove/53-impl--linux-download-consistency-k14.md
index 967164e8b9..b8cec2721e 100644
--- a/.grove/53-impl--linux-download-consistency-k14.md
+++ b/.grove/53-impl--linux-download-consistency-k14.md
@@ -37,3 +37,30 @@ ## Notes
 Do not change production framing/finalization to match a presumed macOS cause.
 If the reusable fixtures are insufficient, narrow the Linux question or
 decompose it; do not reopen or absorb the macOS inquiry.
+
+## Decisions (running log)
+- Source: the Linux handler opens the path once (`system_endpoints.handle_download`),
+  takes the length by `os.fstat` on that descriptor, then reads 1 MiB chunks from the
+  same descriptor (`server._handle_download`). The shipped bundle `server.py` and
+  `system_endpoints.py` equal the checkout. The macOS cause, a second path lookup, has
+  no Linux counterpart in source. The cases test the descriptor seams instead.
+- Fixture: one fresh container of the pinned k5 image
+  (`python@sha256:dddfd7e0…`, arm64), uid 1000, no network except published loopback
+  ports. Case files live on the container file system, not on a bind mount, so Linux
+  file semantics apply. The Host CLI 2.8.0 reaches the agent through the Docker port.
+  A probe on 2026-11-18 downloaded the oracle bytes this way.
+- Seam: an in-container supervisor runs the unchanged handler class in a subclass
+  that only records writes. It wraps `os.fstat` and the opened file at module
+  boundaries. An armed hook changes the file inside the agent process at the
+  descriptor seam, or pauses until the host resumes it. The writer process differs
+  from production, but rename, truncate and write semantics do not depend on the
+  writing process.
+- Unchanged lane: the stress runs and the immutable publication control use
+  the unchanged `run_server`, with no hooks.
+- No clone: the question is the agent file and HTTP boundary, which the container
+  reaches on a Linux kernel. The guest relay stays untested, as in k7. The report
+  states this limit.
+- 2026-11-18. Trials 1 and 2 (scratchpad, not retained) corrected the seam before any recorded run. The writer wrapper needed `closed` and `close`, because `StreamRequestHandler.finish` uses both. A killed agent left its `paused` marker, so `prepare` now removes stale markers and reports them. A restarted agent continues the request numbering, so `emitted-N.bin` names stay unique. The abandon client did not send its request.
+- 2026-11-18. The checker is independent of the controller: its own oracle, its own retained-record digest and its own case list. It requires each armed hook to run at its event, and the agent's socket bytes to equal the raw client's bytes. A 64 MiB transfer must stay within 150 s with a peak RSS rise of 16 MiB at most.
+- 2026-11-18. Tasks `diagnostic:linux-download-consistency-{container,check,tools}` were verified with forced failures: an unused import fails `tools`, a deleted record and an extended `received.bin` fail `check`, and an existing OUT is refused with exit 2. The first refusal check was void: the runner lacked its execute bit and failed with 127. It was repeated after `chmod +x`.
+- 2026-11-18. The evidence keeps bodies up to 8 MiB as files. The 64 MiB body is kept by digest only. A run is about 137 MB, in line with other retained runs here.
diff --git a/Taskfile.yml b/Taskfile.yml
index a0577b62c0..554f3941c1 100644
--- a/Taskfile.yml
+++ b/Taskfile.yml
@@ -507,6 +507,30 @@ tasks:
       - shellcheck download_k7_run.sh
       - uv run --locked python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider test_download_k7.py
 
+  diagnostic:linux-download-consistency-container:
+    desc: 'Run the k14 Linux download consistency cases in one container into a new OUT directory. The container runs the Linux agent from this checkout twice: once with the k14 seam, which traces and changes the file at open, at fstat or before a read, and once unchanged for the stress and publication controls. Raw HTTP and Host CLI downloads are compared with the versions the file held. No VM is used. OUT-outer keeps the identities and the check'
+    requires:
+      vars: [OUT]
+    cmds:
+      - experiments/exec-reliability/download_k14_run.sh {{.OUT | shellQuote}}
+
+  diagnostic:linux-download-consistency-check:
+    desc: 'Judge one retained k14 OUT directory. Classify every raw and Host CLI download, compare the agent bytes with the received bytes, and recheck the file semantics, oracle, stress, publication, streaming, loopback and disposal controls. The result goes to OUT/RESULT, by default check.json. Use another RESULT to re-judge a retained OUT. Exit 0 only when the evidence is complete'
+    requires:
+      vars: [OUT]
+    cmds:
+      - uv run --project experiments/exec-reliability --locked python experiments/exec-reliability/download_k14_check.py {{.OUT | shellQuote}} {{.RESULT | default "check.json" | shellQuote}}
+
+  diagnostic:linux-download-consistency-tools:
+    desc: Check the k14 Linux download consistency controller, seam, checker, runner and controls
+    dir: '{{.ROOT_DIR}}/experiments/exec-reliability'
+    cmds:
+      - uv run --locked python -m ruff check download_k14.py download_k14_seam.py download_k14_check.py test_download_k14.py
+      - uv run --locked python -m ruff format --check download_k14.py download_k14_seam.py download_k14_check.py test_download_k14.py
+      - uv run --locked python -m pyright download_k14.py download_k14_seam.py download_k14_check.py test_download_k14.py
+      - shellcheck download_k14_run.sh
+      - uv run --locked python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider test_download_k14.py
+
   diagnostic:deadline-stall-tools:
     desc: Check the k154 worker-deadline control tooling
     dir: '{{.ROOT_DIR}}/experiments/exec-reliability'
